<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Penn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1644">1644</a>–<a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Penn was an English Quaker and the founder of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. Son of the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-william-penn">Sir William Penn</a>, he became a trustee of West Jersey and a proprietor of East Jersey, and in 1681 Charles II granted him a vast colony west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> to settle a debt owed to his father. He planned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and guaranteed religious freedom; in 1682 he also received the Lower Counties that became Delaware.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-penn.jpg" alt="William Penn" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Penn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Penn was born on 24 October 1644 at Tower Hill, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. His mother, Margaret Jasper, was a Dutchwoman, widow of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sea captain and daughter of a rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rotterdam">Rotterdam</a> merchant. His father served in the Commonwealth Navy, was rewarded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> with Irish estates and later took part in the restoration of Charles II. Penn was educated at Chigwell School, by private tutors in Ireland and at Christ Church, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he arrived in 1660. Though his father's position made him a Cavalier, his sympathies lay with the persecuted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a>, and he was expelled from the university in 1662 for religious nonconformity.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> he began attending Quaker meetings, was arrested, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> at the age of 22. His furious father ordered him out of the house and withheld his inheritance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Penn became a prolific Quaker writer. In 1668, after his tract The Sandy Foundation Shaken, he was held in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on a charge of publication without a licence, though the real accusation was blasphemy. He wrote No Cross, No Crown in 1669 while in jail, and answered the charge of denying the Trinity in Innocency with her open face. In 1677 a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> including Penn bought West Jersey, and East Jersey followed in 1682.</p><p>Seeing conditions worsen for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Quakers</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Penn proposed mass emigration. In 1681 Charles II granted him land to offset debts owed to his father, and the next year Penn sailed up Delaware Bay and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. He framed a government on Quaker ideals of peace, equality and representative government. His land was also claimed by Lord Baltimore, and the dispute with the Calverts ran for eighty years. Penn returned to England in 1684 and went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> in December 1699, finding it grown to nearly 18,100 inhabitants, with over 3,000 in Philadelphia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his later years Penn was in financial trouble. His agent Philip Ford cheated him, and after Ford died in 1702 his widow Bridget claimed title to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>; Penn was held in debtors' prison, then under house arrest, until Quaker friends paid the claim.</p><p>He died on 10 August 1718, aged 73, at Ruscombe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berkshire">Berkshire</a>, and is buried at Jordans near Chalfont St Giles. After his death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> drifted from a colony founded on religion towards a commercial, secular state, but many of his legal and political ideas took root, as did the Quaker school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> for which he issued charters in 1689 and 1701. Delegates to the 1787 Constitutional Convention were inspired by the principles of his Frame of Government. A statue of Penn tops Philadelphia City Hall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “William Penn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>West Jersey and Pennsylvania were established by <b>William Penn</b> in 1676 and 1682, and he signed a peace treaty with Tamanend, leader of the Lenape; the peace endured almost a century, until the Penn's Creek Massacre of 1755.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The Quaker <b>William Penn</b>, founder of the Province of Pennsylvania, employed an anti-militarist public policy, and the colony was essentially unarmed for the 75 years from 1681 to 1756 and experienced little or no warfare.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pacifism">Pacifism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote><b>William Penn</b> reached the Delaware River aboard the Welcome to take possession of his new colony.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-27">27 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1682 James granted the three counties of New Castle, Kent and Sussex to <b>William Penn</b>, who governed them with the Province of Pennsylvania.</blockquote><a href="/state/delaware-colony">Delaware Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>After Carteret's death his heirs sold the colony in 1682 to 24 proprietors, many of them Quakers and Scots, including <b>William Penn</b> and Robert Barclay.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-jersey">East Jersey</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1681 Charles II granted <b>William Penn</b> a vast tract west of the Delaware River to settle a debt owed to Penn's father.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-pennsylvania">Province of Pennsylvania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Ruscombe, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1692" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Penn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1692">What was here in 1692? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Certainly, if the Common Law be so hard to be understood, it's far from being very Common…&#8221;</p><footer>At his trial at the Old Bailey, September 1670 &#183; original &#183; as the printed account of the trial (1670) records it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7360/pg7360-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Governments, like clocks, go from the motion men give them; and as governments are made and moved by men, so by them they are ruined too. Wherefore governments rather depend upon men, than men upon governments.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let men be good, and the government cannot be bad; if it be ill, they will cure it. But, if men be bad, let the government be never so good, they will endeavor to warp and spoil it to their turn.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Any government is free to the people under it (whatever be the frame) where the laws rule, and the people are a party to those laws, and more than this is tyranny, oligarchy, or confusion.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for liberty without obedience is confusion, and obedience without liberty is slavery.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
